"subtopic": "Magnets",
"difficulty": 2,
"kw": "",
"stem": "<p>Alice placed a bar magnet over some steel paper clips. She recorded the number of steel paper clips attracted to the different parts of the bar magnet, X, Y and Z.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/EOY_ACSP/q29_fig1.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A bar magnet is labelled X (left end), Y (middle part) and Z (right end), with a tray of steel paper clips below it.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The results are shown in the graph below.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/EOY_ACSP/q29_fig2.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bar chart \"number of steel paper clips attracted\" vs \"parts of bar magnet\": two bars, in the middle and on the right, with three empty boxes below the bars to be labelled.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>(a) Label the bar graph with the correct parts X, Y and Z. [1]<br>(b) Explain your answer for (a). [1]<br>(c) Alice conducted an experiment to find out which bar magnet is the strongest. She hung four magnets of equal lengths, A, B, C and D at different distances above a tray of metal pins. The results are shown below.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/EOY_ACSP/q29_fig3.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Four magnets of equal length, A, B, C and D, are hung at different heights: C is the highest (the pins it attracts hang highest/furthest), while B, A and D are lower.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Alice concluded that magnet C is the strongest magnet. Do you agree with her? Explain your answer. [2]</p>",
"options": [],
"myAnswer": "(a) 从左到右依次标注：Y（无柱/0 枚）、X、Z。\n  (b) A magnet's attraction is the strongest at its two poles (ends X and Z), so most clips are attracted at X and Z and none at the middle Y.\n  (c) Yes. Magnet C is the furthest from the tray of pins and can still attract (the most number of) pins, so it has the strongest magnetic pull.",
"answer": "(a) Boxes from left to right: Y, X, Z (no bar at Y).\n  (b) A magnet's attraction is the **strongest at its two poles**.\n  (c) Yes, magnet C is the **furthest** from the tray of pins and can still attract the (most number of) pins.",
"keypoints": "(b) \"the magnet is strongest at its two ends (poles)\" (strongest at the two poles/ends)\n(c) ① \"Yes/Agree\"\n(c) (2) \"C is the furthest from the drawing pin (furthest) but can still attract it\"",
