"marks": 5.0,
"topicRaw": "Magnets",
"topic": "Interactions",
"subtopic": "Magnets",
"difficulty": 3,
"kw": "",
"stem": "<p>The diagram below shows a way to separate steel scraps and rubber chips. A mixture of steel scraps and rubber chips was poured onto the moving rubber belt and collected in containers A and B.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/EOY_ACSP/q31_fig1.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The mixture is poured onto a rubber belt that runs over a roller with a magnet; the steel filings are held by the magnet and travel further with the belt, while the rubber bits fall off earlier; containers A and B are below.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>(a) State which containers, A or B, will collect the steel scraps and rubber chips. [1]<br>(b) Explain how the steel scraps and rubber chips are separated into containers A and B. [2]<br>(c) The magnet was replaced with a much stronger magnet. Predict and explain how the collection of steel scraps and rubber chips will be affected. [2]</p>",
"options": [],
"myAnswer": "(a) Steel scraps: A. Rubber chips: B.\n  (b) The rubber chips are non-magnetic materials so they will not be attracted to the magnet and fall into B. The steel scraps are magnetic materials so they will be attracted to the magnet and will not fall into B but into A instead.\n  (c) Less steel scraps will be collected in A. They will be attracted to the magnet for a longer time and drop later (some steel scraps may be carried past A).",
"answer": "(a) Steel scraps: Container A. Rubber chips: Container B.\n  (b) The rubber chips are **non-magnetic** materials so it will not be attracted to the magnet and falls in B. The steel scraps are **magnetic** materials so it will be attracted to the magnet and will not fall into B but into A instead.\n  (c) **Less** steel scraps will be collected. They will be attracted to the magnet for a **longer** time and drop later.",
