"myAnswer": "- (a) Group G: Flowering plants; Group H: Non-flowering plants.\n  - (b) No. The plant may be a flowering plant that is not flowering at the time / has not grown flowers yet (e.g., a young plant). Not seeing flowers does not mean it can never flower.",
"answer": "- a) Group G: Flowering plant; Group H: Non-flowering plant.\n  - b) No. Plants need sunlight to grow. So this plant has weak stem and needs support to reach out for sunlight in order to grow.\n  - ⚠️ Note: The official (b) answer matches the question asked (whether all plants without flowers are non-flowering plants) rather loosely — the official answer actually explains why the plant in the diagram has a weak stem and needs support (plants need sunlight; weak-stemmed plants need support to reach towards sunlight). My answer fits the question better; combining it with the official answer, the key scoring points are: a plant cannot be judged non-flowering merely because \"no flower is seen\" (it may not have flowered yet / its flowering season has not arrived), and weak-stemmed plants need support to obtain sunlight.",
