"marks": 3.0,
"topicRaw": "Light & Shadows",
"topic": "Energy",
"subtopic": "Light & Shadows",
"difficulty": 3,
"kw": "",
"stem": "<p>Jenny blows air into a balloon made of material H.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/WA2_MahaBodhi/q10_fig1.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Comparison of a deflated balloon before blowing air in and an inflated balloon after blowing air in.</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> (i) State a suitable material for material H.　[1]</p>\n<p>(ii) What happens to the size of the balloon after air was blown in?　[1]</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Jenny then used a torchlight and shone a light through material H as shown below.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/WA2_MahaBodhi/q10_fig2.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Set-up for a light-passing experiment: torchlight → material H → screen.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>She then repeated the experiment using another material and made the following observations.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"images/WA2_MahaBodhi/q10_fig3.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Table: Material H — A blur spot of light was seen on the screen.; Material J — No light was seen on the screen.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Based on her observations above, what can she conclude about materials H and J?　[1]</p>",
"options": [],
